:{"className":"crays-article-deck"},"children":["Relays Are Where the Network Gets Real in the relay layer: storage, discovery, access, policy, uptime and the boring server details that decide whether Nostr feels alive."]},"\n          "]},"\n          ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{"className":"crays-nostr-hero-visual","style":{"--nostr-hero-visual-position":"center"}},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"figure","attributes":{},"children":["\n              ",{"tag":"img","attributes":{"src":"/assets/nostr-free-stock/relays-visual.jpg","alt":"Relays Are Where the Network Gets Real visual","loading":"eager","decoding":"async"},"children":[]},"\n            "]},"\n            ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{"className":"crays-nostr-hero-signal"},"children":["\n              ",{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["Relays"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["Deep dives"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"small","attributes":{},"children":["Part of the Crays Nostr knowledge base."]},"\n            "]},"\n          "]},"\n        "]},"\n      "]},"\n\n\n\n\n      ",{"tag":"section","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-main"},"children":["\n        ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-reader-shell crays-article-layout"},"children":["\n          ",{"tag":"aside","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-toc","aria-label":"Article sections"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Reading path"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#the-server-did-not-disappear"},"children":["The server did not disappear"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#a-relay-has-a-personality-whether-it-admits-it-or-not"},"children":["A relay has a personality whether it admits it or not"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#the-outbox-idea-is-a-rescue-attempt"},"children":["The outbox idea is a rescue attempt"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#running-a-relay-is-a-product-decision"},"children":["Running a relay is a product decision"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#what-a-reader-should-know"},"children":["What a reader should know"]},"\n          "]},"\n          ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-content"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"header","attributes":{"className":"crays-nostr-article-masthead"},"children":["\n              ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{"className":"crays-nostr-article-masthead__meta"},"children":[{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["Relays"]},{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["12 min read"]},{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["Deep dives"]}]},"\n              ",{"tag":"h2","attributes":{"className":"crays-nostr-article-masthead__title"},"children":["Relays Are Where the Network Gets Real"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{"className":"crays-nostr-article-masthead__deck"},"children":["Relays are not background plumbing. They are where cost, policy, speed, spam, geography and memory show up."]},"\n            "]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Nostr can sound serverless if you explain it too quickly. It is not. Relays are servers. They accept events, store what they choose to store and send events back to clients. The difference is that no single relay owns the whole network. That distinction matters, but it does not make servers vanish."]},"\n\n              ",{"tag":"h2","attributes":{"id":"the-server-did-not-disappear"},"children":["The server did not disappear"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Nostr can sound serverless if you explain it too quickly. It is not. Relays are servers. They accept events, store what they choose to store and send events back to clients. The difference is that no single relay owns the whole network. That distinction matters, but it does not make servers vanish."]},"\n              ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Once you see relays clearly, a lot of Nostr behavior stops feeling mysterious. Missing notes, slow feeds, paid access, spam filtering, local rooms, archive gaps and discovery problems often begin at the relay layer. The relay is where the open protocol meets electricity bills and human policy."]},"\n\n              ",{"tag":"h2","attributes":{"id":"a-relay-has-a-personality-whether-it-admits-it-or-not"},"children":["A relay has a personality whether it admits it or not"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Some relays want to be broad public infrastructure. Some want to be paid and quiet. Some serve communities. Some focus on search, media, local use, archives or experiments. Some disappear. Some reject events. Some become spam magnets. Some are run by people with taste, patience and a higher tolerance for weird logs than most of us possess."]},"\n              ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["That is why relay lists are not just technical settings. They are part of the social map. If your client writes to one set of relays and your friend reads from another, the network can feel broken even when both of you are doing something reasonable."]},"\n\n              ",{"tag":"h2","attributes":{"id":"the-outbox-idea-is-a-rescue-attempt"},"children":["The outbox idea is a rescue attempt"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Relay selection becomes painful when everyone has to guess where everyone else publishes. Outbox-style discovery tries to make that less chaotic by helping clients know where to look for a user's events. The point is simple: the network needs better memory of where people actually write."]},"\n              ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["This is not glamour work. It is the kind of infrastructure that makes ordinary users stop blaming themselves. When discovery works, the client feels obvious. When it fails, the user thinks Nostr is empty, broken or too hard."]},"\n\n              ",{"tag":"h2","attributes":{"id":"running-a-relay-is-a-product-decision"},"children":["Running a relay is a product decision"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["A relay operator has to decide what to store, what to reject, whether to charge, how to fight spam, how long to keep data, what logs to keep, what legal risk to tolerate and which community they are actually serving. Those choices shape user experience even when the interface hides them."]},"\n              ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["For venues and communities, this becomes even more interesting. A local relay can support a room, an event, a membership circle or a place-based social layer. But local infrastructure needs local responsibility. You do not get the romance of a local graph without the work of operating it."]},"\n\n              ",{"tag":"h2","attributes":{"id":"what-a-reader-should-know"},"children":["What a reader should know"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["You do not need to run a relay to use Nostr. You do need to know that relays affect reach, reliability and memory. If a client lets you inspect or choose relays, treat that as a feature worth learning slowly. If a client hides relay behavior, ask whether it still gives you good defaults and honest failure messages."]},"\n              ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Relays matter because Super Nodes, venues, archives and community rooms are not theoretical. The relay layer is where Nostr becomes operational: less mystical, more useful, and much easier to inspect."]},"\n\n"]},"\n        "]},"\n      "]},"\n    "]},"\n\n\n  ",{"tag":"footer","attributes":{"className":"legal-footer crays-nostr-footer","aria-label":"Crays footer"},"children":["\n    ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{"className":"legal-footer-inner"},"children":["\n      ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{"className":"legal-footer-top"},"children":["\n        ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{},"children":["\n          ",{"tag"